  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 149 PM CDT Wed Aug 12 2026\n\nThe threat for dangerous heat stress conditions continues to be\nour greatest message by far. The subtropical ridge building, \nexpanding, and intensifying overhead as we move into the weekend \nwill be quite strong. 500 mb heights of 597 to 598 dam are just \nbelow records for mid August. Subsidence beneath the ridge and \nincreased sunshine will continue to dry and bake the South into \nnext week. As the ridge strengthens, some of the humidity will be \neased, but actual air temperatures will begin to push or exceed \n100 degrees. The combination of heat and humidity will ensure most\ndays see peak heat index values in the 105-110 degree range \nthrough the weekend.\n\nLocal temperature climatology studies suggest that a range of \n850mb temperatures of 21-24C would support high temperatures \npretty reasonably in the 97 to 103 degree range. Official forecast\nvalues are on the upper end of this range, so would conservatively \nsay that at least parts of our forecast area will hit the 100 \ndegree mark during the next 7 days, and a few locations may reach\n103, or hot enough to set record highs. Deeper mixing would push \nthe dew points slightly lower, but would also feel pretty \nconfident in the 105+ heat index values, especially as recent\nguidance has come in with slightly higher RH forecasts. Will \ncontinue to monitor for potential expansion and increase of \nmessaging to \"Significant\" risk of dangerous heat (110+ heat index\nor 105+ air temperature) as we move into late week and the \nweekend. An areawide Heat Advisory is in effect, and expect \nadditional Advisories or Excessive Heat Warnings to be issued over\nthe coming days.\n\nIn terms of rain chances, they are mostly non-existent until early\nnext week. At that time, there is a signal for deeper moisture in\nthe westerlies north of the high center to circulate around the \nhigh and potentially increase convective rainfall chances over \neastern portions of the forecast area. Rain chances could be\nfurther increased by an approaching frontal boundary, but any \nrelief from the heat that could result is uncertain at this time. \n/NF/EC/",
